<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n33208" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ōtĭōsĭtas</span></b> ,
			ātis, f.
		otiosus, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>leisure</i>, <i>idleness</i> (late
			Lat.). </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<b>Lit.</b>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">multam mahtiam docuit otiositas,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Vulg. Ecclus. 33, 29</span></i>
			. —</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b>Transf.</b>, <i>a writing composed
				at leisure moments</i> (post-class.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">suas otiositates edere,</span>”
				<i>the fruils of leisure</i>, <i>poems</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">Sid. Ep. 2, 10</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
</div>